{"records":[{"id":"m7tpzttyjx","title":"Rapid compensatory evolution can rescue low fitness symbioses following partner-switching","description":"This data accompanies the publication 'Rapid compensatory evolution can rescue low fitness symbioses following partner-switching'. \nAs preprint: https://doi.org/10.1101/2020.11.06.371401\nAs a publication: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2021.06.034","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-13T08:46:14.433Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/m7tpzttyjx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/m7tpzttyjx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Megan","last_name":"Sorensen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jamie","last_name":"Wood","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Duncan","last_name":"Cameron","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael ","last_name":"Brockhurst","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Fitness in Evolutionary Biology","Metabolomics","Flow Cytometry","Evolution Symbiosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nw9yp22mfk","title":"RNAseq analysis results","description":"Results obtained from transcriptional profiling of genes of the isolated yeast strains using RNAseq analysis. Excel file with differential expression of genes and counts per million of yeast genes for the studied strains","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-09T07:42:04.594Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nw9yp22mfk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nw9yp22mfk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"DESPINA","last_name":"LOLA","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Differential Gene Expression","Wine Yeasts"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"v5w4nvh9vx","title":"Data and codes for \"A fitness trade-off explains the early fate of yeast aneuploids with chromosome gains\"","description":"Data and codes for \"A fitness trade-off explains the early fate of yeast aneuploids with chromosome gains\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-22T14:07:11.644Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v5w4nvh9vx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v5w4nvh9vx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Simone","last_name":"Pompei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marco","last_name":"Cosentino Lagomarsino","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Yeast","Data Analysis","Aneuploidy","Bayesian Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r5kz3kj6f2","title":"Supplementary data: Adaptation of the yeast gene knockout collection is near-perfectly predicted by fitness and diminishing return epistasis","description":"Supplementary data tables for “Adaptation of the yeast gene knockout collection is near-perfectly predicted by fitness and diminishing return epistasis”\n\nScan-o-matic growth phenotype datasets of the haploid BY4741 single gene deletion collection (MATa; his3Δ1; leu2Δ0; met15Δ0; ura3Δ0; GeneX::kanMX) adaptation to arsenite stress. From each growth curve, the colony populations doubling times (hours) have been extracted as a proxy for fitness (Zackrisson et al., 2016). \nData S1: reports dose-response growth data for gene deletion strains exposed to different degrees of arsenite stress. Data S2: reports growth data, before LOESS fitting, for the complete collection of BY4741 gene deletion strains during adaptive evolution in 3 mM of arsenite. Data S3: reports growth data, after LOESS fitting, for the complete collection of BY4741 gene deletion strains after 25, 50 and 75 generations of adaptive evolution in 3 mM of arsenite. Data S4: reports growth data, before LOESS fitting, of BY4741 gene deletion strains during adaptive evolution across a range of selection pressures. Data S5: reports growth data, after LOESS fitting, of BY4741 gene deletion strains after 25, 50 and 75 generations across a range of selection pressures. Data S6: reports growth data, of BY4741 gene deletion strains on background media before and after adaptation to a range of selection pressures. Data S7: reports growth data, of single and double mutation strains in Synthetic complete medium, and arsenite, 3 mM.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-03-25T21:28:43.487Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r5kz3kj6f2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r5kz3kj6f2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Karl","last_name":"Persson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Simon","last_name":"Stenberg","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Markus","last_name":"Tamas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jonas","last_name":"Warringer","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Evolutionary Biology","Adaptation","Yeast","Experimental Evolution"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9s29hry5hy","title":"Hsieh et al 2022","description":"The critical role of the INO80 chromatin-remodeling complex in transcription is commonly attributed to its nucleosome sliding activity. Here we’ve found that INO80 prefers to mobilize hexasomes over nucleosomes. INO80’s preference for hexasomes reaches up to ~60-fold when flanking DNA overhangs approach the ~18 bp linkers in yeast gene bodies. Correspondingly, deletion of INO80 significantly affects the positions of hexasome-sized particles within yeast genes in vivo. Our results raise the possibility that INO80 promotes nucleosome sliding by dislodging an H2A/H2B dimer, thereby making a nucleosome transiently resemble a hexasome. We propose that this mechanism allows INO80 to rapidly mobilize nucleosomes at promoters and hexasomes within gene bodies. Rapid repositioning of hexasomes that are generated in the wake of transcription may mitigate spurious transcription. More generally, such versatility may explain how INO80 regulates chromatin architecture during the diverse processes of transcription, replication, and repair. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-04-12T13:15:01.418Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9s29hry5hy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9s29hry5hy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Geeta","last_name":"Narlikar","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Chromatin Remodeling"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vnnf8gtj54","title":"TC-NER and GG-NER in Yeast rDNA - Paillé et al. 2023","description":"Raw Data associated to the Paillé et al (2023) Distinctive participation of transcription coupled- and global genome- nucleotide excision repair of pyrimidine dimers in the transcribed strand of yeast rRNA genes. Biochemestry (in submission process). \n\nHigh resolution mapping of UV induced DNA damage results : \nRepair of pyrimidine dimers at a nucleotide resolution in the rRNA genes regions of WT, rad34 and rad16 yeast strains at promoter, 5'end, 18S and 25S regions of transcribed strand and non-transcribed strand. Yeast were UV irradiated at 180 J/m2 and harvested after 0, 0.5, 1, 2 and 4 hours (hrs).  Isolated DNA from non-irradiated (U) and irradiated cells (0 to 4 hrs) were digested with appropriated restriction enzyme (Figure 1A) and used as template for Taq polymerase primer-extension. Repair of PDs was assessed by the extension of appropriated primers (Figure 1A). ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-06T16:35:58.077Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vnnf8gtj54","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vnnf8gtj54"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Audrey","last_name":"Paillé","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Antonio","last_name":"Conconi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Repair"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sryzrbvt9v","title":"Data for: Effects of developmental acclimation on fitness costs differ  between two aphid species","description":"Experiment 1 : Effects of developmental acclimation on the emergence and CTmax of adults \nExperiment 2 : Effects of acclimation during development on fitness traits ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-09-17T10:30:39.188Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sryzrbvt9v","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sryzrbvt9v"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"zhao","last_name":"fei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Huiping","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xing","last_name":"Kun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Junyu","last_name":"Cao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Insect Physiology","Insect Ecology"],"journal_issn":["03064565"]},{"id":"7fk6n46gh8","title":"The TSC22D, WNK, and NRBP gene families exhibit functional buffering and evolved with Metazoa for cell volume regulation","description":"The ability to sense and respond to osmotic fluctuations is critical for the maintenance of cellular integrity. We used gene co-essentiality analysis to identify an unappreciated relationship between TSC22D2, WNK1 and NRBP1 in regulating cell volume homeostasis. Each of these genes have paralogs and are functionally buffered for osmo-sensing and cell volume control. Within seconds of hyperosmotic stress, TSC22D, WNK and NRBP family members physically associate into biomolecular condensates, a process that is dependent on intrinsically disordered regions (IDRs). A close examination of these protein families across metazoans revealed that TSC22D genes evolved alongside a domain in NRBPs that specifically binds to TSC22D proteins, which we have termed NbrT (NRBP binding region with TSC22D), and this co-evolution is accompanied by rapid IDR length expansion in WNK family kinases. Our study reveals that TSC22D, WNK and NRBP genes evolved in metazoans to co-regulate rapid cell volume changes in response to osmolarity. \n\nThe uploaded files include two additional movies for endogenous mScarlet-TSC22D2 upon hyperosmotic stress and AF-M2 predictions for TSC22D paralogs binding with NRBP paralogs.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-07-11T17:09:41.969Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7fk6n46gh8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7fk6n46gh8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"YUXI","last_name":"XIAO","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Canada Research Chairs Program","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h531d29","name":"Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council","grant_id":"RGPIN-2022-04849"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":"PJT-185992"},{"id":null,"name":"Genome Canada and Ontario Genomics","grant_id":"OGI-139"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":"PJT-463531"},{"id":null,"name":"Ontario Research Fund","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Cell Biology","Structural Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"44tr2ch3pc","title":"comparing the effects of water-based and land-based exercise on cardiovascular fitness","description":"comparing the effects of water-based and land-based exercise on cardiovascular fitness","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-20T06:12:58.99Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/44tr2ch3pc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/44tr2ch3pc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Haifeng","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Meta-Analysis","Cardiovascular Function","Exercise Therapy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3sdsc83vsn","title":"Genome-Scale Exon Perturbation Screens Uncover Exons Critical for Cell Fitness","description":"CRISPR-Cas technology has transformed functional genomics, yet understanding how individual exons differentially shape cellular phenotypes remains limited. Here, we optimized and conducted massively parallel exon deletion and splice site mutation screens in human cell lines to identify exons that regulate cellular fitness. Fitness-promoting exons are prevalent in essential and highly expressed genes, and commonly overlap with protein domains and interaction interfaces. Conversely, fitness-suppressing exons are enriched in non-essential genes, exhibiting lower inclusion levels, and overlap with intrinsically disordered regions and disease-associated mutations. In-depth mechanistic investigation of the TAF5 alternative exon-8, screen hit, revealed that its inclusion is required for assembly of the TFIID general transcription initiation complex, thereby regulating global gene expression output. Collectively, our orthogonal exon perturbation screens established a comprehensive repository of phenotypically important exons and uncovered regulatory mechanisms governing cellular fitness and gene expression.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-20T08:55:47.576Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3sdsc83vsn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3sdsc83vsn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mei-sheng","last_name":"Xiao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Arun Prasath","last_name":"Damodaran","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bandana","last_name":"Kumari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ethan","last_name":"Dickson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kun","last_name":"Xing","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tyler","last_name":"On","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nikhil","last_name":"Parab","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Helen E. ","last_name":"King","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexendar R.","last_name":"Perez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wilfried M. ","last_name":"Guiblet","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gerard","last_name":"Duncan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anney","last_name":"Che","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Raj","last_name":"Chari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thorkell","last_name":"Andresson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joana A.","last_name":"Vidigal","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robert J.","last_name":"Weatheritt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Aregger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thomas","last_name":"Gonatopoulos-Pournatzis","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":121637,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":130,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":121637,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":121186,"restricted":60,"embargoed":391},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2040},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1324},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":549},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":441},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":416},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":383},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":381},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":338},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":273},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":246}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":119077},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1889},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":174},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":126},{"id":"df228bb